---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { LAST_UPDATED } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
import { displayDate } from '../../lib/format';
import { P, type SourceKey } from '../../lib/engine/params';
import * as E from '../../lib/engine/counciltax';
import { helpers } from '../../lib/helpers';
const lang = 'en';
const h = helpers();
const { m2, pc } = h;
const s = (k: SourceKey) => P.sources[k];
const L = (k: SourceKey, t?: string) => `<a href="${s(k).url}" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">${t ?? s(k).label.en}</a>`;
const B = P.bands;
const ratios = (n: E.Nation) => B[n].letters.map((l, i) => `${l} ${B[n].num[i]}/${B[n].den}`).join(', ');
const hl = E.bill({ council: 'highland', band: 0 });
const hlOne = E.bill({ council: 'highland', band: 0, household: 'one' });
const hlDis = E.bill({ council: 'highland', band: 0, disabled: true });
const bham = E.bill({ council: 'birmingham', band: 1 });
const ex = E.instalmentPlan(bham.annual, 10);
const W = P.scottishWater;
const counts = { e: E.inNation('england').length, w: E.inNation('wales').length, sc: E.inNation('scotland').length };
---
<PageLayout title="Council Tax Method 2026/27: Tables, Rules, Tests and Limits" description="How Bill by Band works out a 2026/27 council tax bill: the official Band D tables, band ratios of each nation, discounts, exemptions, premiums, Scottish Water and instalments." lang={lang} jsonLd={[articleSchema({ title: 'How the council tax bills are worked out', description: 'Method of Bill by Band', path: route('method', lang), lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'Method', path: route('method', lang) }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Method' }]}>
  <header class="mb-6"><p class="kicker">Method</p><h1 class="mt-1 text-3xl font-bold text-navy-900 md:text-4xl">How the council tax bills are worked out</h1></header>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <p>Every calculator on the site calls one engine, a set of pure functions fed by two files: the council figures, generated by a script from the official spreadsheets, and a parameter file holding the national rules for {P.taxYear} ({P.taxYearLong}). No amount is typed into a page. Both files were last checked against their sources on {displayDate(P.retrieved_at, 'en-GB')}.</p>
    <h2>1. The Band D figure of each council</h2>
    <p>England: {counts.e} billing authorities, from Table 9 and Table 10 of <Fragment set:html={L('mhclg_levels')} />. Table 9 gives the "area council tax for a dwelling occupied by two adults", parish precepts averaged across the council; Table 10 splits the Band D figure between the billing authority, its parishes, the county council or the Greater London Authority, the police and crime commissioner, the fire authority and, where one sets a precept, the combined authority. Last year's figure comes from the 2025 to 2026 edition of Table 9. Wales: {counts.w} councils, from <Fragment set:html={L('wales_levels')} />, with the county, community council and police elements. Scotland: {counts.sc} councils, from the Council Tax by band table of <Fragment set:html={L('scot_datasets')} />, which excludes water and sewerage.</p>
    <p>The script that turns the spreadsheets into data, <code>scripts/data/build-councils.py</code>, checks that Table 9's Band D equals the sum of Table 10's parts for every English council, and that it finds exactly {counts.e}, {counts.w} and {counts.sc} councils. It can be run again on next year's files.</p>
    <h2>2. From Band D to your band</h2>
    <p>A band is a fixed share of Band D, set in section 5 of the Local Government Finance Act 1992 (<Fragment set:html={L('leg_lgfa_ratios')} />). England: {ratios('england')}. Wales: {ratios('wales')}. Scotland since {B.scotland.ratioChange}: {ratios('scotland')} (<Fragment set:html={L('leg_scot_ratios')} />). The charge is rounded to the penny, as councils print it. For all {counts.e} English councils the eight charges computed this way match Table 9 to within a penny, and the tests check it on every build.</p>
    <h2>3. Who lives there</h2>
    <p>The bill assumes two adults. If only one adult counts, it is reduced by {pc(P.discounts.singlePerson)}; if nobody counts, by {pc(P.discounts.allDisregarded)} (<Fragment set:html={L('leg_lgfa_discounts')} />). People who do not count, the "disregarded", are listed in Schedule 1 of the 1992 Act: full-time students and student nurses, apprentices and young people on funded training, under-18s, 18 and 19 year olds at school or college, live-in carers, the severely mentally impaired, diplomats and some others (<Fragment set:html={L('leg_lgfa_disregards')} />, <Fragment set:html={L('gov_who_pays')} />). A home where everyone is a full-time student is exempt, not discounted (<Fragment set:html={L('leg_exempt')} />, <Fragment set:html={L('gov_students')} />). The calculator asks how many adults count; it does not test each person against the schedule for you.</p>
    <h2>4. Disabled Band Reduction</h2>
    <p>A home adapted for a disabled resident is charged at the band below (<Fragment set:html={L('leg_disabled')} />, <Fragment set:html={L('gov_disabled')} />). In Band A the charge falls to five sixths of Band A, which is 5/9 of Band D in England and Wales and 200/360 in Scotland: in Highland the Band A council tax drops from {m2(hl.councilTax)} to {m2(hlDis.councilTax)}, the figure the Highland Council publishes. The reduction is applied before any discount, as on a real bill.</p>
    <h2>5. Second homes and empty homes</h2>
    <p>England: from {h.date(P.premiums.england.secondHomeFrom)} a council may charge up to {pc(P.premiums.england.secondHomeMax)} more on a furnished home that is nobody's main residence; on a home empty and substantially unfurnished for {P.premiums.england.emptyFromYears} year or more (since {h.date(P.premiums.england.emptyFromDate)}), up to 100 %, then 200 % after 5 years and 300 % after 10 years (<Fragment set:html={L('leg_lgfa_premium')} />, <Fragment set:html={L('mhclg_premiums')} />, <Fragment set:html={L('gov_second_empty')} />). Wales: up to {pc(P.premiums.wales.max)} on both since {h.date(P.premiums.wales.from)}, after one year for an empty home (<Fragment set:html={L('wales_premiums')} />). Scotland: since {h.date(P.premiums.scotland.from)} the national default is {pc(P.premiums.scotland.default)}, and each council may set a higher or lower rate, or none (<Fragment set:html={L('scot_circular_2026')} />). The national tables do not say which council charges what, so the calculator starts from the legal maximum in England, 100 % in Wales and the national default in Scotland, and lets you type your council's rate. Exceptions such as a home for sale, probate or job-related accommodation are not modelled.</p>
    <h2>6. Scottish Water</h2>
    <p>Every Scottish bill also collects Scottish Water's charges, the same in every council area: for {W.year} the Band D water charge is {m2(W.water[3])} and the waste water charge {m2(W.sewerage[3])}, up {pc(W.increase)} (<Fragment set:html={L('scot_water')} />). They use their own ratios ({W.ratioNum.join(', ')} over {W.ratioDen}), so Bands E to H are lighter than for council tax. The single person discount and the Disabled Band Reduction apply to them too; a premium does not. Checked against <Fragment set:html={L('highland_charges')} />: Band A for two adults {m2(hl.annual)}, for one adult {m2(hlOne.annual)} (the council prints £1,143.15, a penny of rounding), and with the reduction {m2(hlDis.annual)}.</p>
    <h2>7. Instalments</h2>
    <p>Unless you ask otherwise, the bill is paid in 10 monthly instalments; in England and Wales you have the right to ask for 12 (<Fragment set:html={L('leg_instalments')} />, <Fragment set:html={L('gov_paying')} />). Every instalment except the first is the total divided by the number of instalments, rounded to the nearest pound, and the first takes what is left. A Band B home in Birmingham at {m2(bham.annual)} is paid as {m2(ex.first)} then nine of {m2(ex.regular)}. We apply the same arithmetic to Scotland, where councils set their own schedules. Part of a year is charged by the day, the annual bill divided by {P.daysInYear}.</p>
    <h2>8. Tests</h2>
    <p>Among the cases checked on every build: every English band charge against Table 9; Highland's 2026 to 2027 table for Band A (two adults, one adult, Disabled Band Reduction) with water and waste water; the Scottish ratio of Band H; Band I in Wales at 21/9; the England premium caps at 1, 5 and 10 years; the instalment rounding of the 1992 regulations. A failing test stops the build.</p>
    <h2>9. What the calculator does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Council Tax Reduction for people on a low income, which each council runs with its own scheme in England (see <Fragment set:html={L('gov_ctr')} />);</li>
      <li>the exact parish or community council of your address: we use the average of the council area, so a bill in a parish with a high precept can be a few tens of pounds higher;</li>
      <li>local discounts a council may grant, such as for care leavers, and the exceptions to premiums;</li>
      <li>special expenses charged in part of a district, and Northern Ireland, which has domestic rates instead of council tax.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Method version: {LAST_UPDATED}. A figure that looks wrong: <a href={route('contact', lang)}>contact</a>.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
